\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/nature26159\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/nature26159?error=cookies_not_supported&code=ebed0673-e73b-460b-b99d-840efb71ff1a; Total lines: 468\nL0:   * cite0†View all journals L1:   * cite1†Saved research L2:   * cite2†Search L3:   * cite3†Account†my-profile.springernature.com cite4†Log in†idp.nature.com L4: \nL5:   * cite5†Content Explore content L6:   * cite6†About the journal L7:   * cite7†Publish with us L8:   * cite8†Subscribe L9: \nL10:   * cite9†Sign up for alerts†journal-alerts.springernature.com L11:   * cite10†RSS feed L12: \nL13:   * Article\nL14:   * Published: 28 March 2018\nL15: # The logic of single-cell projections from visual cortex\nL16: \nL17:   * cite11†Yunyun Han ^{cite12†1 ,cite13†2 ,cite14†3 }^{ cite15†na1 },\nL18:   * cite16†Justus M. Kebschull ^{cite17†4 ,cite18†5 }^{ cite15†na1 },\nL19:   * cite19†Robert A. A. Campbell ^{cite14†3 }^{ cite15†na1 },\nL20:   * cite20†Devon Cowan ^{cite14†3 },\nL21:   * cite21†Fabia Imhof ^{cite14†3 },\nL22:   * cite22†Anthony M. Zador ^{cite18†5 }^{ cite23†na2 } &\nL23:   * …\nL24:   * cite24†Thomas D. Mrsic-Flogel ^{cite14†3 ,cite25†6 }^{ cite23†na2 }\nL25: Show authors\nL26: \nL27: cite26†Nature volume 556, pages 51–56 (2018)\nL28: \nL29: cite27†Save article L30: \nL31: cite1†View saved research L32:   * 38k Accesses\nL33: \nL34:   * 337 Citations\nL35: \nL36:   * 259 Altmetric\nL37: \nL38:   * cite28†Metrics details L39: ## Abstract\nL40: Neocortical areas communicate through extensive axonal projections, but the logic of information transfer remains poorly understood, because the projections of individual neurons have not been systematically characterized. It is not known whether individual neurons send projections only to single cortical areas or distribute signals across multiple targets.\nL41: Here we determine the projection patterns of 591 individual neurons in the mouse primary visual cortex using whole-brain fluorescence-based axonal tracing and high-throughput DNA sequencing of genetically barcoded neurons (MAPseq). Projections were highly diverse and divergent, collectively targeting at least 18 cortical and subcortical areas. Most neurons targeted multiple cortical areas, often in non-random combinations, suggesting that sub-classes of intracortical projection neurons exist.\nL42: Our results indicate that the dominant mode of intracortical information transfer is not based on ‘one neuron–one target area’ mapping. Instead, signals carried by individual cortical neurons are shared across subsets of target areas, and thus concurrently contribute to multiple functional pathways.\nL43: This is a preview of subscription content, cite29†access via your institution†wayf.springernature.com L44: ## Access options\nL45: \nL46: cite29†Access through your institution†wayf.springernature.com L47: \nL48: Access Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\nL49: \nL50: Get Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\nL51: \nL52: $32.99 / 30 days\nL53: \nL54: cancel any time\nL55: \nL56: cite30†Learn more†shop-amers.nature.com L57: \nL58: Subscribe to this journal\nL59: \nL60: Receive 52 print issues and online access\nL61: \nL62: $199.00 per year\nL63: \nL64: only $3.83 per issue\nL65: \nL66: cite8†Learn more L67: \nL68: Buy this article\nL69: \nL70:   * Purchase on SpringerLink\nL71:   * Instant access to the full article PDF.\nL72: \nL73: USD 39.95\nL74: [Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL75: \nL76: [Input]\nL77: \nL78: Prices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout\nL79: ### Additional access options:\nL80: \nL81:   * cite4†Log in†idp.nature.com L82:   * cite31†Learn about institutional subscriptions†www.springernature.com L83:   * cite32†Read our FAQs†support.nature.com L84:   * cite33†Contac